# Who may do what, by the name in the client's certificate (ADR-0017).

# The Gateway reads every edge's samples and every edge's declaration of its own drivers, and
# writes each edge's configuration: what an edge reads is derived in the cloud and delivered to it,
# never typed into the edge by hand (ADR-0019).
user scada-gateway
topic read scada/edge/+/samples
topic read scada/edge/+/drivers
topic write scada/edge/+/config
# A write the cloud asks for, and the edge's answer to it (ADR-0023). The Gateway may ask any edge
# and read any edge's answer: it is one conversation per write, matched by the id in the payload,
# so reading them all on one subscription costs nothing and a per-edge filter could not be written
# before the edge exists.
topic write scada/edge/+/writes
topic read scada/edge/+/write-results

# Every edge writes its own samples topic, its own declaration, and its own answers to the writes
# the cloud asks it for; and reads only its own configuration and its own write requests. %u is the
# name in its certificate, which is its Edge:Id. An edge that publishes under another edge's name —
# a declaration or a write result, which is why these are patterns and not topics — or reads
# another edge's configuration or write requests, is refused here, and the refusal is written to
# the audit file.
pattern write scada/edge/%u/samples
pattern write scada/edge/%u/drivers
pattern write scada/edge/%u/write-results
pattern read scada/edge/%u/config
pattern read scada/edge/%u/writes
